# IOCTL(2)

## NAME

ioctl, stty, gtty - control device

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <sgtty.h>

int ioctl(int fildes, int request, char *argp);

int stty(int fildes, struct sgttyb *argp);

int gtty(int fildes, struct sgttyb *argp);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Ioctl* performs a variety of functions on character special files (devices). The writeups of
various devices in section 4 discuss how *ioctl* applies to them.

For certain status setting and status inquiries about terminal devices, the functions *stty*
and *gtty* are equivalent to

> | **ioctl(fildes, TIOCSETP, argp)**
> | **ioctl(fildes, TIOCGETP, argp)**

respectively; see tty(4).

The following two calls, however, apply to any open file:

> | **ioctl(fildes, FIOCLEX, NULL);**
> | **ioctl(fildes, FIONCLEX, NULL);**

The first causes the file to be closed automatically during a successful *exec* operation; the
second reverses the effect of the first.

**Note: stty and gtty are real system calls here, not the ioctl write-arounds v7 made them**.
They have gate numbers of their own and generated stubs of their own, and have had since the
syscall stubs first landed.

## SEE ALSO

stty(1), tty(4), exec(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Zero is returned if the call was successful; -1 if the file descriptor does not refer to the
kind of file for which it was intended.

## BUGS

Strictly speaking, since *ioctl* may be extended in different ways to devices with different
properties, *argp* should have an open-ended declaration like

> **union { struct sgttyb** ...**;** ... **} \*argp;**

## BESM-6 NOTES

**struct sgttyb is two words, not five.** A **char** *member* of a struct occupies one byte at
a byte-granular offset, so the four chars fill bytes 0 through 3 of the first word, bytes 4 and
5 are the trailing pad, and *sg_flags* is the second word. It is a whole *object* that rounds
up to a word, not a member; reading the first rule as though it covered the second is a mistake
this tree has made four times, and this page was one of them. See section 4 of
doc/Besm6_Data_Representation.md, whose numbers were measured. **struct tchars** is six chars
in six bytes: exactly one word, no pad.

**Note:** *ioctl*, *stty* and *gtty* are real under b6sim as well as under the kernel. b6sim
translates between **struct sgttyb** and the host terminal's settings, so a program that clears
ECHO or asks for RAW gets what it asked for and a program that asks whether it is on a terminal
is told the truth. Two differences remain, and both are the machine underneath rather than the
simulation: the console this kernel drives is ECHO|CRMOD|XTABS where a host terminal is
whatever the user left it, and *sg_ispeed*/*sg_ospeed* read back **B0** on the kernel - a
Consul-254 has no baud rate - where b6sim reports what the host line really runs at. A program
that needs the same answer in both worlds must not ask those questions.

The important thing is that the size is fixed by `struct sgttyb`.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_ioctl, SYS_stty, SYS_gtty** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments
where it reads them: 1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the
accumulator, the error number in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
